.SH NAME
.PP
auto-fix-date - Synchronize an incorrect system clock

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-fix-date
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
None
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-fix-date
command corrects the system date.  Services such as ntpd(8)
will fail to correct a clock that has strayed too far, since they will
only make small incremental corrections in order to avoid confusing
other processes.

The ntpdate(8) command instantly corrects the system clock, but
cannot run while ntpd(8) is running.
The auto-fix-date script is a convenience that temporarily
stops ntpd(8), runs ntpdate(8), and restarts ntpd(8).

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), ntpd(8), ntpdate(8)

